The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
msg = (struct ems_cpc_msg *)&ibuf[start];
...
if (start > urb->transfer_buffer_length) {
```
Reason:
The parser uses `urb->transfer_buffer_length` (the maximum buffer size allocated by the driver) instead of `urb->actual_length` (the actual number of bytes received) to validate message boundaries. This can allow the loop to continue reading beyond the valid data. Additionally, before accessing `msg->type` and `msg->length`, there is no check that the message header (`CPC_MSG_HEADER_LEN`) fits within the received data, so a short URB can cause out-of-bounds reads.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
if (start + CPC_MSG_HEADER_LEN > urb->actual_length) {
    netdev_err(netdev, "format error\n");
    break;
}
...
if (start > urb->actual_length) {
```
Reason:
The added check at the beginning of each message ensures that the header of the next message fits within the actual received data before any access. The existing boundary check is corrected to use `urb->actual_length`, so that the loop stops exactly at the end of the received data, preventing out-of-bounds reads and memory corruption.

